% SchwingNet/refs_corrections.bib -- corrected and added entries from the reference audit (REFS_AUDIT.md), re-run
% 2026-09-24 against live records. refs.bib was only read. Keys are unchanged, so each entry below replaces the entry of
% the same key in refs.bib; four entries are additions (castellvi1984, ctsp1kdescriptor, figueroa2012, and the
% optional liu2021ctpelvic1k), and liebrand2026 is in refs.bib but uncited. Entries the audit found OK (konin2010, yun2018, manbachi2018, peh1999, huang2022, verse2021) or
% marks REMOVE (schwing2026mixed) are not repeated.
%
% Every field was checked on 2026-09-24 against the record its DOI resolves to: the doi.org handle API; Crossref for
% publisher DOIs (and the Crossref book records for the two LNCS volumes' editors); DataCite for 10.5281 (Zenodo) and
% 10.48550 (arXiv) DOIs; PubMed E-utilities (esearch by [doi], then efetch) for PMID, NLM journal abbreviation,
% volume, issue, pages and authors. Where a number in the manuscript rests on the full text, the open full text was
% read (see REFS_AUDIT.md, "Claims checked against the source").
%
% Conventions, so that the PDF (biblatex, nejm.bbx) and the submitted docx (pandoc, packet_build/nlm.csl) print the
% same thing:
%  - journal = the NLM title abbreviation (PubMed ISOAbbreviation). Both renderers print the field as stored.
%  - PMIDs sit in the note of @article entries, which neither renderer prints (nejm.bbx replaces note+pages for
%    articles; nlm.csl has no note). For @misc and @inproceedings, nejm.bbx DOES print note, so identifiers and
%    remarks for those types are in % comments only.
%  - addendum carries "Published <date>" / "Posted <date>" for online-only items. nejm.bbx prints it; nlm.csl does
%    not. Access dates are not filled in: take them from the download logs, do not guess them.
%  - Lower-case name particles and proper names in titles are braced, so neither renderer reorders or lowercases them.
%
% refs.bib says it is generated by tools/make_refs.py from paper/rsnai/refs.bib, "entries verbatim". Apply these
% there too, or the next make_refs run restores the errors.

% =====================================================================================================================
% A. Cited entries with substantive errors
% =====================================================================================================================

